1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Ancient China saw — a year in a
shadow.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The shortest day, the longest shadow.
Before calendars, how did people know it
was today? With a stick.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
From one longest shadow to the next: one
year, 365¼ days.

4
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
So the sky got 365¼ degrees: the Sun
moves one a day.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
China’s Shoushi calendar later took
365.2425 days. So does Europe’s
Gregorian calendar, 300 years on.

6
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,000
At noon, a stick’s shadow is the longest
of the year. Tonight, the nearly full
Moon sits by the Pleiades.

7
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Norse Yule: a wolf’s jaw in the sky.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

